"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,340\nEpisode 4\n\n3\n00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:09,746\nLeft brain, right brain\n\n4\n00:00:12,546 --> 00:00:15,933\nLogical, careful, good with numbers: left brained.\n\n5\n00:00:16,255 --> 00:00:19,508\nCreative, intuitive, artistic: right brained.\n\n6\n00:00:19,793 --> 00:00:21,731\nMany people know which one they are.\n\n7\n00:00:22,024 --> 00:00:23,376\nBut is anyone either?\n\n8\n00:00:23,546 --> 00:00:25,963\nThe idea grew from real science.\n\n9\n00:00:26,268 --> 00:00:31,494\nIn the 1960s, Roger Sperry and colleagues studied split brain patients,\n\n10\n00:00:31,757 --> 00:00:34,663\nwhose two hemispheres had been surgically separated.\n\n11\n00:00:35,071 --> 00:00:37,232\nEach side showed different strengths.\n\n12\n00:00:37,570 --> 00:00:40,309\nSperry later shared a Nobel Prize.\n\n13\n00:00:40,632 --> 00:00:43,778\nPopular books stretched this into personality types.\n\n14\n00:00:43,948 --> 00:00:50,973\nIn 2013, Jared Nielsen and colleagues at the University of Utah tested the idea directly.\n\n15\n00:00:51,569 --> 00:00:56,043\nThey analyzed resting brain scans from one thousand and eleven people,\n\n16\n00:00:56,387 --> 00:00:58,186\naged seven to twenty nine,\n\n17\n00:00:58,496 --> 00:01:01,549\nacross more than seven thousand brain regions.\n\n18\n00:01:01,719 --> 00:01:03,929\nThey found lateralized connections,\n\n19\n00:01:04,122 --> 00:01:06,864\nbut no left brained or right brained people.\n\n20\n00:01:07,391 --> 00:01:11,399\nLateralization looked like a local property of particular networks,\n\n21\n00:01:11,612 --> 00:01:13,275\nnot a whole brain type.\n\n22\n00:01:13,445 --> 00:01:17,226\nThe grain of truth: some functions do lean to one side.\n\n23\n00:01:17,653 --> 00:01:20,105\nFor most people, language leans left,\n\n24\n00:01:20,141 --> 00:01:21,629\nand attention leans right.\n\n25\n00:01:22,244 --> 00:01:24,845\nBut real tasks, from math to painting,\n\n26\n00:01:24,998 --> 00:01:27,591\ndraw on both hemispheres working together.\n\n27\n00:01:27,761 --> 00:01:29,576\nThe label is not harmless.\n\n28\n00:01:29,897 --> 00:01:33,790\nA student who decides they are right brained may give up on math.\n\n29\n00:01:34,186 --> 00:01:37,841\nOne who decides they are left brained may never try to draw.\n\n30\n00:01:38,257 --> 00:01:42,106\nAnd the label distracts from what actually builds both kinds of skill: practice,\n\n31\n00:01:42,413 --> 00:01:43,975\nwith feedback.\n\n32\n00:01:44,145 --> 00:01:45,223\nDo this instead.\n\n33\n00:01:45,674 --> 00:01:47,977\nAvoid hemisphere labels in your teaching,\n\n34\n00:01:48,008 --> 00:01:50,841\nincluding quizzes that sort students into types.\n\n35\n00:01:51,190 --> 00:01:56,149\nTalk about skills, which can be practiced,\n\n36\n00:01:56,623 --> 00:01:58,481\nrather than brain sides,\n\n37\n00:01:58,735 --> 00:02:00,044\nwhich sound fixed.\n\n38\n00:02:00,382 --> 00:02:03,200\nOne tip to share with students: don't label yourself; both halves of your brain work on every task.\n\n39\n00:02:03,370 --> 00:02:04,848\nYou are not half a brain.\n\n40\n00:02:05,030 --> 00:02:06,637\nNeither are your students.\n\n"